Let L be the event "winning the game wearing an AoLS", R the event
"winning the game wearing an AoR" and N the event "winning the game
without an AoLS". If it were true that it makes no difference to the
probability of winning with or without an AoLS, then:

P(L) = P(N)
R is a proper subset of N => P(R) < P(N) = P(L)

and it's always less likely to win with an AoR than with an AoLS even
if you never burn the AoLS. On the other hand if P(L) < P(N) then it's
possible that P(L) < P(R) < P(N).

